## Critique of the approach

**The core problem:** `Messaging.sendEmail` is limited to **10 invocations per transaction**. Calling it once per opportunity inside a loop will throw `LimitException` on the 11th call, meaning only 10 out of 200 owners would ever be notified.

**Why the "one call per email" pattern is unnecessary:** You stated the rationale was "so that if one email fails the rest still go out." But `Messaging.sendEmail` already provides this isolation. When you pass an array of emails, the API sends each one independently and returns a `SendEmailResult[]` where each element has its own `isSuccess()` / `getErrorMessage()`. A single rejected email does **not** block the others in the same call. So there is no reliability benefit to calling it 200 times — only a governor-limit catastrophe.

**The fix:** Build all `SingleEmailMessage` objects in the loop, then make a **single** `Messaging.sendEmail` call with the full array (up to 1,000 emails per call is allowed; 200 is well within that).

